  "function_name": "rotate",
  "hints": [
    "Transpose the matrix, then reverse every row.",
    "The transpose swaps matrix[i][j] with matrix[j][i] only for j greater than i, so each pair swaps once.",
    "Reversing each row turns the transpose into a clockwise rotation."
  ],
  "approaches": [
    {
      "name": "Allocate a new matrix",
      "complexity": {
        "time": "O(n^2)",
        "space": "O(n^2)"
      },
      "explanation": "The cell at (i, j) moves to (j, n - 1 - i)."
    },
    {
      "name": "Transpose and reverse",
      "complexity": {
        "time": "O(n^2)",
        "space": "O(1)"
      },
      "explanation": "The in-place rotation."
    }
  ],
  "editorial": "A clockwise rotation sends (i, j) to (j, n - 1 - i). Transposing sends (i, j) to (j, i), and reversing the row then sends that cell to (j, n - 1 - i). Doing the swap only above the diagonal keeps the transform in place.",
  "reference_solution": "from typing import Dict, List, Optional, Tuple\nfrom collections import Counter, OrderedDict, defaultdict, deque\nimport heapq\n\ndef rotate(matrix: list[list[int]]) -> list[list[int]]:\n    n = len(matrix)\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(i + 1, n):\n            matrix[i][j], matrix[j][i] = matrix[j][i], matrix[i][j]\n    for row in matrix:\n        row.reverse()\n    return matrix\n",
  "starter_code": "from typing import List\n\ndef rotate(matrix: List[List[int]]) -> List[List[int]]:\n    pass\n",
